.\" Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-sonnet-5-5
.Dd July 26, 2026
.Dt JOTAIN-COMPLETION-DESIGN 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jotain-completion-design
.Nd completion and snippets — design specification
.Sh DESCRIPTION
\fBDate:\fP 2026-07-26 \fBStatus:\fP Implemented 2026-07-27 in
.Ql "lisp/init-completion.el"
and
.Ql "lisp/init-snippets.el" ,
with coverage in
.Ql "test/completion-test.el" .
Both items recorded as blocking were settled by measurement (§2.3, §2.4).
Where the implementation departs from the specification as first written, the section says so instead of being quietly edited to match.
\fBEvidence base:\fP
.Ql "docs/reviews/2026-07-completion-research.7" .
Section references below of the form \fI(research §2.1)\fP point into that report.
Claims marked \fB[unverified]\fP here are ones the research explicitly could not establish — they are design intent, not evidence.
.Sh 1. REQUIREMENTS
These are the owner's stated requirements.
They are inputs, not conclusions.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| # | Requirement |
| R1 | Completion pops up automatically in code, and only on request in prose (text/org/markdown/commit buffers). |
| R2 | TAB indents. Only. TAB never triggers completion. (Superseded 2026-09-04 — TAB now indents and completes; see §7. Restore with jotain-completion-free-tab.) |
| R3 | A separate, explicit key drives the snippet + completion chain. |
| R4 | RET never accepts a completion. Enter inserts a newline, always. |
| R5 | Sources that matter: eglot (LSP), dabbrev/file, tempel snippets, history/abbrev/spelling. AI inline suggestions are surveyed only — no adoption decision. |
| R6 | Every feature here has a documented opt-out. |
.Ed
.Pp
Two prior decisions are settled and not revisited: \fBtempel\fP is the snippet engine, and the AI layer gets \fBno recommendation\fP either way.
.Sh 2. DESIGN
.Ss 2.1 Trigger model (R1)
\fBGlobal default: no auto-popup.\fP
.Ql "corfu-auto"
stays
.Ql "nil"
— which is also corfu's own shipped default (research §4.1).
.Pp
\fBCode buffers opt in\fP, per-mode, before the mode activates:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(add-hook 'prog-mode-hook (lambda () (setq-local corfu-auto t)))
.Ed
.Pp
This works with
.Ql "global-corfu-mode"
for a specific, verified reason:
.Ql "corfu-auto"
is read \fBexactly once\fP, in the
.Ql "corfu-mode"
body at enable time, and
.Ql "global-corfu-mode"
dispatches from
.Ql "after-change-major-mode-hook" ,
which
.Ql "run-mode-hooks"
runs \fIafter\fP major-mode hooks (research §4.1).
The value latches;
.Ql "corfu-auto--post-command"
never re-reads it.
.Pp
Two failure modes this design must respect, both verified:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "corfu-mode-hook"
is too late.\fP
.Ql "define-minor-mode"
runs the mode hook after the body.
Setting
.Ql "corfu-auto"
there does nothing.
.It
\fBBuffers already open when
.Ql "global-corfu-mode"
first enables keep their old value\fP — they do not re-run major-mode hooks.
Relevant at startup ordering and after a config reload.
A buffer opened before corfu initialised will not have auto-popup until its mode is re-run.
.El
.Pp
\fBProse buffers get nothing extra.\fP They inherit the global
.Ql "nil" ,
so completion there is manual-only via the key in §2.3.
No
.Ql "text-mode-hook"
entry is needed — absence \fIis\fP the configuration.
.Pp
\fBScoping:\fP upstream's own per-mode knob is
.Ql "global-corfu-modes" ,
which toggles
.Ql "corfu-mode"
itself, not auto-vs-manual.
The
.Ql "setq-local" Ns No "-in-a-hook"
technique is \fIa\fP mechanism the corfu README licenses (\(dqsetting
.Ql "corfu-auto"
to t locally … before enabling\(dq), not one upstream presents as \fIthe\fP prog/prose recipe (research §4.1).
.Pp
\fBTuning.\fP
.Ql "corfu-auto-delay"
and
.Ql "corfu-auto-prefix"
keep their current values
.No \&( Ns Ql "0.1"
/
.Ql "2" )
pending measurement.
The research found \fBno evidence\fP for any particular tuning (research §6.6), so don't present these values as tuned.
\fI(Moved to
.Ql "0.2"
/
.Ql "3"
in §6.2.)\fP
.Ss 2.2 TAB (R2)
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "tab-always-indent"
=
.Ql "t" Ns No ".\fP"
.Pp
This is the \fBstock Emacs default\fP on both 30 and 31, and
.Ql "indent-for-tab-command" Ns No "'s"
only
.Ql "completion-at-point"
call is guarded by
.Ql "(eq tab-always-indent 'complete)" ,
making that branch provably unreachable (research §2.1).
The current config sets
.Ql "'complete"
.No \&( Ns Ql "lisp/init-completion.el" Ns No ":276);"
this design reverts to the default.
.Pp
The claim that corfu recommends
.Ql "'complete"
— i.e. that this deviates from upstream — \fBwas refuted 0-3\fP (research §2.1).
.Pp
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "tab-first-completion"
is out of scope.\fP It was added in Emacs \fB28.1\fP (not 30), takes exactly five fixed symbols (not a predicate), and is \fBinert unless
.Ql "tab-always-indent"
is
.Ql "complete" Ns No "\fP"
(research §2.3).
It should be documented as irrelevant here, never tuned.
This retires the \(dqguard\(dq option considered during scoping.
.Pp
\fBTAB must also be freed inside the popup.\fP
.Ql "corfu-map"
binds
.Ql "TAB"
→
.Ql "corfu-complete"
(research §6.5).
Setting
.Ql "tab-always-indent"
alone leaves TAB completing whenever the popup is open, which violates R2 in exactly the moment it matters most:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(keymap-unset corfu-map "TAB" t)
(keymap-unset corfu-map "<tab>" t)
.Ed
.Pp
\fBSuperseded — there is no exception.\fP This section originally kept
.Ql "TAB"
for snippet field navigation as the one deliberate exception.
That exception is gone: fields now move on
.Ql "M-}" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "M-{"
(tempel's own keys) or
.Ql "C-M-n" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "C-M-p" ,
and
.Ql "TAB"
appears nowhere.
.Pp
The exception rested on a false premise: \fBupstream
.Ql "tempel-map"
never bound
.Ql "TAB" Ns No "\fP."
It ships
.Ql "M-}" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "M-{" ,
.Ql "M-RET"
.No \&( Ns Ql "tempel-done" ),
.Ql "M-<up>" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "M-<down>"
and some command remaps; the
.Ql "TAB" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "S-TAB"
pair was this configuration's own addition in
.Ql "init-snippets.el" .
Removing it let tempel's own keys take over.
.Pp
.Ql "C-M-n" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "C-M-p"
are added alongside as a mnemonic alias.
No field key may collide with corfu's
.Ql "M-n" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "M-p" :
.Ql "tempel-map"
is installed as an overlay
.Ql "keymap"
property, which is consulted \fIbefore\fP minor-mode maps, so a shared key would be stolen from the popup mid-snippet.
.Ql "M-["
would have been actively hostile on a TTY —
.Ql "ESC ["
is the CSI introducer.
.Pp
Other TAB consumers that bypass
.Ql "tab-always-indent"
entirely and are \fBnot\fP touched by this design (research §2.2):
.Ql "org-cycle" ,
.Ql "message-tab" ,
shell-mode's TAB,
.Ql "c-tab-always-indent" ,
and the minibuffer.
.Ss 2.3 The keys (R3, R4)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| Key | Role |
| C-M-i | The chain key. Opens completion; inside the popup, inserts the selected candidate. |
| TAB | Indent. Only. (The snippet-field exception was dropped, §2.2; TAB completes since §7.) |
| RET | Newline. Only. Never accepts a candidate. |
| M-n / M-p | Navigate candidates. |
| C-g | Dismiss. |
| M-+ / M- | tempel by name / interactive insert (unchanged). |
.Ed
.Pp
\fBWhy
.Ql "C-M-i"
and not
.Ql "M-TAB" Ns No ".\fP"
The Emacs manual directs users away from
.Ql "M-TAB"
because window managers reserve Alt+Tab, and recommends
.Ql "C-M-i"
or
.Ql "ESC TAB"
(research §2.4).
On a TTY all three collapse to the same event, so
.Ql "C-M-i"
is the only choice that is safe on GUI \fIand\fP terminal — and this repo ships a terminal-only distribution.
.Pp
Binding a chain command to
.Ql "C-M-i"
\fBoverrides a documented standard binding\fP (global
.Ql "C-M-i"
→
.Ql "complete-symbol"
→
.Ql "completion-at-point" ).
That is a deliberate, documented choice, not a defect.
Note
.Ql "C-u C-M-i"
currently runs
.Ql "info-complete-symbol" ;
a custom command replaces that behavior.
.Pp
\fBRET (R4).\fP Upstream documents freeing it explicitly (research §6.5):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(keymap-unset corfu-map "RET" t)
.Ed
.Pp
This is a supported configuration, not a workaround.
If shell/comint buffers later need RET to send input, upstream's
.Ql "menu-item"
.Ql ":filter"
form is the sanctioned pattern.
.Pp
\fBResolved — and it turned on a mechanism, not a preference.\fP
.Ql "corfu-map"
contains a
.Ql "<remap> <completion-at-point>"
entry, and a remap fires only for the command the key actually \fIresolves to\fP.
Binding
.Ql "C-M-i"
to
.Ql "completion-at-point"
— rather than leaving the stock global
.Ql "complete-symbol" ,
which is not remapped — is therefore what makes the same key both open the popup and accept inside it.
No second accept key is needed, and the choice of
.Ql "completion-at-point"
is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
.Pp
The remap is repointed from corfu's default
.Ql "corfu-complete"
to
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "corfu-insert" Ns No "\fP."
.Ql "corfu-complete"
only extends the common prefix (insert status
.Ql "exact" ),
so it neither commits the highlighted candidate nor runs a capf's
.Ql ":exit-function"
— so a tempel snippet name accepted with
.Ql "C-M-i"
would not expand.
.Ql "corfu-insert"
inserts the selected candidate with status
.Ql "finished" :
it commits the candidate and expands snippets, which is the \(dqfinish the completion\(dq gesture R3 wants.
.Pp
Asserted in
.Ql "completion-test-one-key-opens-and-accepts" .
.Pp
This also settled the open question about how the accept gesture \fIfeels\fP: with
.Ql "corfu-preview-current nil"
and the default
.Ql "corfu-preselect"
.No \&( Ns Ql "valid" ),
nothing is selected until you navigate with
.Ql "M-n" ,
so
.Ql "C-M-i"
extended the prefix and appeared to do nothing.
The lever named here is now pulled —
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "corfu-preselect"
is set to
.Ql "first" Ns No "\fP"
so the top candidate is always selected and highlighted (via the
.Ql "corfu-current"
face), giving
.Ql "corfu-insert"
something to commit and showing the user what
.Ql "C-M-i"
will insert.
.Ql "first"
is safe precisely because RET/TAB are freed: only the explicit
.Ql "C-M-i"
commits, so it cannot cause the accidental accept
.Ql "first"
risks when RET/TAB accept.
Asserted in
.Ql "completion-test-corfu-preselects-first" .
.Ss 2.4 The chain (R3)
The chain is \fBdeclarative, via capf ordering\fP — not a custom dwim command.
This falls out of two verified facts:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ql "tempel-expand"
returns \fBonly the single exactly-matching template\fP and presents no UI (research §6.1).
.It
Both tempel capfs return
.Ql ":exclusive 'no" ,
so they \fBfall through\fP when nothing matches (research §1.3, §6.1).
.El
.Pp
So ordering
.Ql "tempel-expand"
ahead of the semantic capf yields exactly the desired behavior — \fIexpand a snippet if the text before point names one, otherwise complete normally\fP — with
.Ql "C-M-i"
bound to plain
.Ql "completion-at-point" :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
prog-mode buffer, buffer-local completion-at-point-functions:
  tempel-expand           (depth -90)   exact template name → expand, else fall through
  eglot-completion-at-point (depth 0)   semantic candidates
  t                                     → global list: cape-keyword, cape-file, cape-dabbrev
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql "tempel-complete"
(all templates, shown in the popup) remains available on
.Ql "M-+"
for browsing by name.
.Pp
\fBPrior art:\fP
.Ql "make-hippie-expand-function"
is the sanctioned way to build a bespoke chain bound to its own key, and hippie-exp's Commentary explicitly recommends several such functions \(dqbound to different keys\(dq (research §6.4).
This design reaches the same end through the capf list instead, which keeps a single
.Ql "completion-at-point"
entry point and avoids a second, parallel expansion mechanism.
.Ql "hippie-expand"
is \fBnot\fP adopted.
.Pp
\fBResolved by measurement — and it exposed a live bug.\fP All four facts below are asserted in
.Ql "test/completion-test.el" ;
none came from documentation.
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
\fBAn exclusive capf shadows everything after it, unconditionally\fP — even when nothing it offers matches the text at point.
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "cape-capf-nonexclusive"
is the fix.\fP Wrapping the exclusive capf restores the fallthrough, while still keeping control when its own candidates match.
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "cape-capf-super"
propagates non-exclusivity only when \fIevery\fP input is non-exclusive.\fP One exclusive input makes the whole merge exclusive.
.It
\fBThe
.Ql ":exclusive no"
fallthrough ignores
.Ql "completion-styles" Ns No ".\fP"
It is decided by a bare
.Ql "try-completion" ,
so
.Ql "foo"
is discarded against
.Ql "barfoo"
under orderless exactly as under
.Ql "basic"
— even though orderless itself matches.
The
.Ql "minibuffer.el"
FIXME is real, not theoretical.
.El
.Pp
\fBFacts 1 and 3 together were a live bug.\fP
.Ql "init-snippets.el"
merges the snippet capf with eglot's.
Tempel is non-exclusive but eglot is not, so the merged capf inherited eglot's exclusivity — meaning
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "cape-dabbrev" ,
.Ql "cape-file"
and
.Ql "cape-keyword"
never ran in any LSP buffer.\fP They were configured and dead.
The implementation wraps the merged capf in
.Ql "cape-capf-nonexclusive" ,
gated by
.Ql "jotain-completion-eglot-nonexclusive" .
.Pp
\fBFact 4 bounds a residual weakness\fP, which the merge happens to mitigate.
A non-exclusive capf loses candidates a non-prefix pattern would have matched.
For
.Ql "tempel-complete"
alone that would mean snippet names vanishing whenever you type an orderless-style pattern.
Inside the merge the collection also holds the server's candidates, so the prefix test generally succeeds and the merged capf is kept.
This is a real argument for the merge that the original rationale never made — but it is mitigation, not a fix, and the underlying approximation is upstream's.
.Pp
\fBDeparture from the specification as written.\fP §2.4 originally proposed replacing
.Ql "tempel-complete"
with
.Ql "tempel-expand"
(exact-match, no popup entry).
The implementation keeps
.Ql "tempel-complete"
and the merge, on the owner's decision that snippet names should stay visible in the popup.
.Ql "tempel-expand"
would have been structurally immune to fact 4 — it never hands the wrapper a partial prefix — so that immunity was traded for discoverability, knowingly.
.Pp
\fBCorrection to the current config's stated rationale.\fP
.Ql "lisp/init-snippets.el" Ns No ":45-48"
says prepending
.Ql "tempel-complete"
would shadow LSP candidates.
It would not — tempel is non-exclusive and falls through (research §1.3).
The
.Ql "cape-capf-super"
merge is still defensible, but for a \fIdifferent\fP reason than the comment gives: it makes snippet and server candidates appear in \fBone\fP popup simultaneously, rather than the server's list appearing only when no template matches.
If that simultaneity is not wanted, the merge can be dropped in favour of plain ordering — which also sheds cape's documented whole-lifetime candidate cache, currently used with \fBno cache buster\fP (research §1.5).
.Ss 2.5 Prose completion (R1, R5)
Manual-only, via
.Ql "C-M-i" .
Sources in a prose buffer:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ql "tempel-expand"
/
.Ql "tempel-complete"
(text-mode hook, already configured).
.It
.Ql "cape-dabbrev" ,
.Ql "cape-file" ,
.Ql "cape-keyword"
from the global list.
.It
\fBNot\fP
.Ql "ispell-completion-at-point" :
.Ql "init-writing.el" Ns No ":42"
sets
.Ql "text-mode-ispell-word-completion"
to
.Ql "nil" .
The research confirms this removes exactly two things — the (default-absent)
.Ql "C-M-i"
binding and the buffer-local ispell capf — and nothing else (research §5.4).
The existing rationale (the capf throws \(dqNo plain word-list found\(dq where
.Ql "ispell-alternate-dictionary"
is absent, as on NixOS) stands, and jinx covers spell-checking.
.El
.Pp
Worth recording: had that option been left at its default, the ispell capf sits at \fBdepth 10\fP and would have been a harmless late fallback, not a shadow (research §5.1).
.Pp
\fBDocumented hazard.\fP If
.Ql "flyspell-mode"
is ever enabled,
.Ql "flyspell-mode-map"
binds
.Ql "M-TAB" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "ESC TAB" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "C-M-i"
to
.Ql "flyspell-auto-correct-word" ,
and as a minor-mode map it \fBoutranks the global binding\fP — silently destroying the chain key in exactly the buffers prose completion is for (research §5.6).
This config uses jinx, not flyspell, so it should not arise; the mitigation if it ever does is
.Ql "(setopt flyspell-use-meta-tab nil)" .
.Ss 2.6 AI / LLM inline suggestions (R5)
\fBNo recommendation is made, per the stated constraint.\fP
.Pp
Both research rounds produced \fBzero verified claims\fP on the AI completion layer — copilot.el, minuet-ai, codeium/windsurf, aidermacs, gptel, eca-emacs — and on how ghost-text overlays interact with corfu's child frame or with core
.Ql "completion-preview-mode"
(research §6.6).
Nothing in this document should be read as evidence for or against adoption.
.Pp
One structural note that \fIis\fP verified and would apply to any future adoption: Emacs 31's
.Ql "completion-preview-inhibit-functions"
is the sanctioned per-context suppression hook for core's ghost text (research §3.2), and core's preview binds TAB \fBonly while a preview is displayed\fP (research §3.1) — which would conflict with R2 in that window.
Core
.Ql "completion-preview-mode"
is \fBoff by default\fP and this design does not enable it.
.Sh 3. OPT-OUT DESIGN (R6)
.Ss 3.1 Idiom
\fBGrounded in this repo's own convention, not external evidence.\fP The research gathered nothing on opt-out idioms across two rounds — neither the Elisp manual's normative guidance nor any real-world config (research §6.6).
Rather than invent an evidence claim, this section follows the pattern already established in
.Ql "lisp/" :
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
a
.Ql "defgroup jotain-<area>"
.No \&( Ns Ql "jotain-ui" ,
.Ql "jotain-writing" ,
.Ql "jotain-vc" ,
.Ql "jotain-devops" );
.It
boolean or list
.Ql "defcustom jotain-<area>-<knob>"
toggles with
.Ql ":type"
and
.Ql ":group"
.No \&( Ns Ql "jotain-prog-warn-non-ts-mode" ,
.Ql "jotain-prog-enable-risky-js-lsp" ,
.Ql "jotain-prog-warn-non-ts-exclude" );
.It
set with
.Ql "setopt" ,
per the repo rule.
.El
.Ss 3.2 Proposed knobs
All under a new
.Ql "(defgroup jotain-completion nil … :group 'convenience)"
in
.Ql "lisp/init-completion.el" .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| Option | Default | Disables |
| jotain-completion-auto-modes | '(prog-mode-hook) | Auto-popup. nil ⇒ manual-only everywhere, including code. Add hooks to extend auto-popup to more modes. |
| jotain-completion-auto-delay | 0.1 | — (tuning; see §2.1 on the absence of evidence) |
| jotain-completion-auto-prefix | 2 | — (tuning) |
| jotain-completion-free-return | t | nil ⇒ restore corfu's default RET = corfu-insert. |
| jotain-completion-free-tab | t | nil ⇒ restore corfu's default TAB = corfu-complete. |
| jotain-completion-eglot-nonexclusive | t | nil ⇒ leave the LSP capf exclusive, so the cape fallbacks stay suppressed in LSP buffers (see §2.4). |
| jotain-completion-key | "C-M-i" | The chain key. nil ⇒ bind nothing, leaving the stock global binding intact. |
| jotain-completion-fallbacks | t | nil ⇒ omit cape-dabbrev / cape-file / cape-keyword from the global capf list. |
| jotain-completion-snippets | t | nil ⇒ do not add any tempel capf (M-+/M- still work). |
.Ed
.Pp
\fBGranularity rule:\fP every knob disables exactly one mechanism, and
.Ql "nil"
always means \(dqbehave as stock Emacs/corfu would\(dq.
No knob silently re-enables another.
.Pp
\fBSubstitution, recorded:\fP the ninth knob was originally
.Ql "jotain-completion-snippet-tab" ,
gating the
.Ql "TAB"
field-navigation exception.
That exception no longer exists (§2.2), so the knob would have gated nothing — and its
.Ql "nil"
branch would have meant \(dqrestore a binding upstream never had\(dq, violating the rule above.
It is replaced by
.Ql "jotain-completion-eglot-nonexclusive" ,
which gates a mechanism the implementation genuinely adds and which the original table omitted.
The count is still nine.
.Ss 3.3 Timing semantics — stated, because it is not uniform
This matters and cannot be papered over:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ql "jotain-completion-auto-modes" ,
.Ql "-snippets" ,
.Ql "-fallbacks"
are read at \fBload time\fP (they add/remove hooks).
Changing them needs a \fBrestart\fP, or manual
.Ql "remove-hook" .
.It
.Ql "-auto-delay" ,
.Ql "-auto-prefix"
are read \fBper keystroke\fP — immediate.
.It
.Ql "-free-return" ,
.Ql "-free-tab" ,
.Ql "-snippet-tab" ,
.Ql "-key"
modify keymaps at load time; a
.Ql ":set"
function could make them immediate, at the cost of the
.Ql "setopt" Ns No "-only"
caveat that pattern implies.
.El
.Pp
\fBRecommendation:\fP do \fBnot\fP add
.Ql ":set"
functions in the first implementation.
Keep the knobs load-time and document the restart, rather than shipping half-reactive options.
(The precedent is core's own
.Ql "text-mode-ispell-word-completion" ,
whose
.Ql ":set"
covers the keymap half while the capf half is read at mode entry — a split that demonstrably confused this research, twice.)
.Ss 3.4 Escape hatches that need no knob
Documented for the user, because they cost nothing to support:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
\fBPer-buffer, right now:\fP
.Ql "M-x corfu-mode"
toggles the popup in the current buffer.
.It
\fBAuto-popup in one buffer:\fP
.Ql "M-x eval-expression"
→
.Ql "(setq-local corfu-auto nil)"
then re-run the major mode (the value latches at mode-enable time — §2.1).
.It
\fBEverything off:\fP
.Ql "M-x global-corfu-mode"
toggles the whole stack.
.It
.No "\fB" Ns Ql "var/custom.el"
is write-only\fP in this config, so Customize-set values do not persist across sessions by design.
Opt-outs belong in the user's own init or in a machine-local file, not in Customize.
.El
.Pp
An environment-variable opt-out (precedent:
.Ql "JOTAIN_INFO_DIR" )
is \fBdeliberately not proposed\fP.
That precedent exists for a value that must be readable before Lisp can discover it; completion behavior has no such constraint, and a defcustom is more discoverable.
.Sh 4. DELTA FROM THE CURRENT CONFIGURATION
What implementing this would change, as written before implementation (see Status for what landed).
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| File | Change |
| lisp/init-completion.el:276 | tab-always-indent 'complete → t. |
| lisp/init-completion.el:281-287 | corfu-auto t → nil globally; add prog-mode-hook opt-in. |
